// runtime/root/darwin/bootworker.bit — split out of ./boot.bit (#2882) to
// bring both files under the 800-line limit. Holds the worker OS-thread
// body (workerBody), its bounded join (joinWorker), and the first green
// thread's trampoline (mainTrampoline) — the three things boot() starts
// running on a live OS thread or green task once setup is done. ./boot.bit
// keeps the setup sequence itself (boot()), the BIT_GC_STATS report, the
// bit_rt_map_pages/bit_rt_unmap_pages/bit_rt_spawn seams, and the process
// entry (machoMain).
//
// Same module as every sibling here (module files share one scope), so
// boot.bit's imports and module state — schedBlock, mainTaskBlock,
// bootSleepReq, mainExitCode, mainDone — are visible below with no
// re-import, and boot.bit calls back into workerBody/joinWorker/
// mainTrampoline the same way it always did. Named for readability only:
// the checker types every top-level declaration before any function body,
// so name resolution between sibling files is order-independent either way
// (verified on 838c1dc0; the earlier claim that a forward reference here
// could miscompile was wrong and is withdrawn).

// The worker OS-thread entry — same as ../linux/boot.bit's `workerBody`,
// including its reason for driving the find/drain/step trio in a local loop
// rather than passing the `nowFn`/`pollFn` seam to `schedWorkerRun`: a function
// value is a managed-heap `(code, env)` cell and this module must emit
// `--freestanding` (§17.6, #1628).
//
// THE DRAIN IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND ITS ABSENCE WAS #1673. This loop used to be a
// bare `schedWorkerStepNow`, which probes the three queues and the timer list and
// NOTHING ELSE. So this — the only worker the runtime starts — never called
// `pollDrain`, and a green thread parked on the netpoller by `netAwaitReady` had
// no thread in the process that could ever wake it. It did not hang: the poll fd
// was never created either (see `boot` step 4b), so `netAwaitReady` bailed at its
// `pollFd == 0` guard, `netReadSock` fell through to the unscheduled-caller spin,
// exhausted it, and returned -1 — which `bit_rt_net_read` reports as an EMPTY
// READ, indistinguishable from an orderly peer close. Every socket read that had
// to wait for a packet silently returned "". Both halves are needed and neither
// is sufficient: a poll fd nothing drains parks the reader forever, and a drain
// with no poll fd returns 0 on its first line.
//
// THE ORDER IS `schedFindWorkIdle`'s, which is `runtime/sched/`'s: queues, then
// timers, then — only if all of those came up empty — the poller, whose woken
// tasks land on the global queue, which `schedWorkerStepAt` pops from when the
// drain reports a non-zero wake count. Polling
// only when idle is what keeps a `kevent` syscall off the task-dispatch hot path;
// polling AFTER the timers is what stops a busy fd from starving a sleeper.
//
// `parkMonoNs()` IS THE POINT OF THE LOCAL LOOP (#1648). Timer deadlines are set
// by sibling ./time.bit's `bit_rt_time_sleep_ns` as `parkMonoNs() + ns`, so the
// worker that expires them must read the SAME clock. The previous
// `schedWorkerRunBare` folded the clock away to a constant 0, against which no
// positive deadline is ever due — `println/sleep/println` printed its first line
// and hung forever. Nothing else in this file may supply the clock: it is read
// here, once per turn, and passed straight through.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE IDLE SLEEP (#1665) — kept identical in ../linux/boot.bit
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `runtime/sched`'s `schedWorkerStep` cannot reach a platform sleep for idle
// (that module may import neither park provider, and the providers' pinned
// `bit_rt_port_park_sleep_ns` is not a member of `libbitrt.a`, so `worker.bit`'s
// own comment states it is unreachable there — E0078, verified on both Linux
// triples). Its no-work branch instead burns `spinBackoff`, a busy spin, which
// is why an idle worker pegged a whole core.
//
// `addr == 0 && woke == 0` is EXACTLY `schedWorkerStep`'s own no-work predicate
// — `schedFindWorkAt` found nothing and `pollDrain` woke nothing — and this
// loop already computes both before calling into the step, so the idle case is
// distinguished HERE, by the caller, with no change to `runtime/sched`'s pinned
// `bit_rt_port_sched_worker_step_at` API. This is sound only because this
// runtime starts exactly ONE worker (ABI.md §5): `woke > 0` with nothing to pop
// off the global queue would mean another consumer raced this thread for it,
// which cannot happen here. When work IS found, `schedWorkerStepAt` is called
// exactly as before and runs it.
//
// The sleep is bracketed with `bit_rt_gc_blocking_begin`/`_end` (ABI.md §5),
// `runtime/sched/`'s own idle path and this file's `boot`/`joinWorker` sleeps: no
// frame here holds a live Bit reference while parked, so the collector may pass
// this worker's slot without waiting for a safepoint it will not reach until
// `parkSleepNs` returns. `scStopping` is checked first and by hand, matching
// `schedWorkerStep`'s own order, so a shutdown signalled while idle is still
// seen within one backoff step rather than after a sleep starts.
fn workerBody(arg: *byte): *byte {
  // ARM THIS THREAD'S CRASH DIAGNOSTIC FIRST (#2246, ./signal.bit): every
  // green task this worker will ever run executes below this line, so the
  // alternate signal stack must be live before any of them can fault.
  installAltStackForThisWorker()

  // CLAIM THE MUTATOR SLOT EAGERLY, BEFORE ANY BIT CODE RUNS (#1677/#1699).
  // Second only to arming the crash diagnostic above — installAltStackForThisWorker()
  // allocates nothing and touches no safepoint, so the window this call closes
  // is still shut. This is the thread's ENTRY function — the same place
  // `gcThreadExit` below runs at exit, and `runtime/sched/`'s `Worker.run` calls
  // `gc_hooks.threadEnter()` at the matching point. Lazy registration (claiming
  // a slot on first allocation or first safepoint poll) leaves a real window:
  // `schedWorkerStepAt`'s very first task dispatch can `schedSwitch` into a
  // green task body BEFORE this thread's own loop back edge is ever reached,
  // and #1677's reproducer proved a task that reads a live reference out of
  // shared state before its own first allocation/loop is invisible to the
  // rendezvous for that whole window — the object was swept 20/20 runs.
  // Calling `gcThreadEnter()` here closes it: this thread is a mutator before
  // it ever runs Bit code.
  gcThreadEnter()

  let block = taskAt(int(arg))
  let workerAddr = *block
  let doneAddr = *(block + 1)

  let w = taskAt(workerAddr)
  let s: *i64 = ptrOf(schedBlock)
  let backoff = parkMinBackoffNs
  // WORKER 0 POLLS, THE REST PARK (#1902). The Linux twin, line for line — see
  // `runtime/sched/sleep.bit` for why, and ../linux/boot.bit for the same code.
  // The park itself is `__ulock_wait` here and `futex(2)` there, which is the
  // only difference and is entirely inside `runtime/park/<os>`.
  let parks = *(w + wkId) != 0
  let n = 0
  while (n < WORKER_MAX_STEPS) {
    n = n + 1
    // Arm BEFORE looking for work; see the Linux twin for the window this closes.
    if (parks) {
      schedIdleArm(w)
    }
    let addr = schedFindWorkAt(w, parkMonoNs())
    let woke = 0
    if (addr == 0) {
      woke = pollDrain(int(s), 0)
    }
    if (addr == 0 && woke == 0) {
      if (atomicLoad(s + scStopping) != 0) {
        break
      }
      gcBlockingBegin()
      if (parks) {
        schedIdlePark(s, w)
      } else {
        parkSleepNs(backoff, w + wkSleepReq)
      }
      gcBlockingEnd()
      backoff = parkNextBackoff(backoff)
      continue
    }
    // Running instead of parking: clear the arm so a producer does not spend
    // its one wake on a worker that is already busy.
    if (parks) {
      schedIdleDisarm(w)
    }
    backoff = schedWorkerStepAt(w, addr, woke, backoff)
    // TEARDOWN (#2167). `schedWorkerStep`, inside `schedWorkerStepAt`, already
    // unregistered the retired task from the collector's root set before
    // returning, so its stack and task block are unreachable and safe to
    // release here. `wkLastDone` — not `addr` — names the retired task,
    // because the netpoller-drain path picks its own task internally when
    // `addr == 0` (see `wkLastDone`'s own comment in `runtime/sched/worker.bit`).
    //
    // `mainTaskBlock` IS EXCLUDED, ALWAYS. It is a fixed module array
    // registered through `schedRegisterBigStack`, never `mapPages`'d, and it
    // reaches this identical `taskDone` transition through this identical
    // loop when `bit_main` returns. Unmapping it would `munmap` memory this
    // module never `mmap`'d.
    let doneTask = *(w + wkLastDone)
    if (doneTask != 0 && doneTask != int(ptrOf(mainTaskBlock))) {
      let dt = taskAt(doneTask)
      unmapPages(*(dt + taskStackBase), *(dt + taskStackBytes))
      unmapPages(doneTask, (taskWords + 1) * 8)
    }
    if (backoff == workerStepStop) {
      break
    }
  }

  // RELEASE THE MUTATOR SLOT (#1666). `runtime/sched/`'s `Worker.run` — the oracle
  // this function ports — carries `gc_hooks.threadEnter()` /
  // `defer gc_hooks.threadExit()`, and the exit half was missing here. The
  // worker registers lazily at its first safepoint poll and then died without
  // ever releasing slot 1, which stayed `mRunning` forever; from that moment
  // every rendezvous the boot thread started waited out `stwSpinBound` for a
  // thread that no longer existed and abandoned. `../../gc/gcworld.bit` states
  // the consequence on `gcThreadExit` itself: "a leaked slot stays `running`
  // forever, which corrupts nothing but makes every later rendezvous expire,
  // i.e. collection quietly stops." Measured at ~160 abandoned collections per
  // run regardless of workload size, and 0 with this call.
  //
  // PLACEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is the oracle's: AFTER the run loop, so
  // no Bit code runs on this thread again, and BEFORE the done store, whose
  // tail is raw pointer arithmetic holding no managed reference. That is
  // `gcThreadExit`'s precondition. This is NOT the #1650/#1651 shape — nothing
  // is suppressed while the thread can still run Bit code; the slot is released
  // only once the thread is leaving. Releasing it any earlier would let a
  // rendezvous proceed past a thread that is still running, which is the defect
  // that sweeps live objects.
  gcThreadExit()

  let z: *i32 = nil
  atomicStore(z + doneAddr / 4, i32(0))
  return nil
}

// Trip bound for `joinWorker`, `runtime/thread`'s `JOIN_MAX_POLLS` unchanged, so
// the join is finite even if the deadline clock misbehaves (Power of 10, rule 2).
const joinMaxPolls: int = 100000

// `runtime/thread`'s `threadJoin` for THE one worker this runtime starts, with
// the platform clock and sleep called directly instead of taken as function
// values (see `workerBody`). Identical to ../linux/boot.bit's.
fn joinWorker(done: *i32, timeoutNs: int): bool {
  // A non-positive timeout still gets one look at the word, so a join on an
  // already-finished worker succeeds rather than reporting a spurious timeout.
  if (atomicLoad(done) == i32(0)) {
    return true
  }
  let deadline = parkMonoNs() + timeoutNs
  let backoff = parkMinBackoffNs
  let i = 0
  while (i < joinMaxPolls) {
    if (atomicLoad(done) == i32(0)) {
      return true
    }
    if (parkMonoNs() >= deadline) {
      return false
    }
    parkSleepNs(backoff, ptrOf(bootSleepReq))
    backoff = parkNextBackoff(backoff)
    i = i + 1
  }
  return false
}

// The first green thread: run `bit_main`, publish its exit code, leave the task.
fn mainTrampoline() {
  mainExitCode = int(bit_main())
  atomicStore(ptrOf(mainDone), i32(1))
  schedTaskExit()
}

